From 304f09dc8b51f5fc07175b81f13aaa5d59fcc1c7 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: ObeseCatLord <6099645+ObeseCatLord@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 20 Oct 2026 10:25:15 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] iop: classify the remaining shutdown latches; guest errors no
 longer disable the IOP (H-IOP4, H-IOP7, H-IOP14)

Inventory of every markResetRequired/throwing path, each now either kept with
a stated reason or converted (policy comment next to markResetRequired):

Converted to errors / ignored-and-logged:
- SPU2 bus errors a guest can cause (byte access to the 16-bit registers,
  unaligned halfword/word access, DMA sync mode other than block mode, a DMA
  range that is not owned aligned IOP RAM, a second transfer on a busy core,
  empty/oversized/unsupported AutoDMA blocks) throw SpuBusError, are caught at
  the bus, ignored (reads return 0, a refused DMA clears its start bit) and
  logged with a rate limit. Genuine device faults still latch.
- an AutoDMA completion and an ordinary transfer's completion on one core are
  the same IRQ line: they now coalesce into one interrupt that still settles
  the ordinary transfer. Replacing the older entry used to leave the device
  with a transfer nobody settled, so the next DMA on that core was refused and
  the IOP latched (H-IOP14 AutoDMA overwrite).
- an incomplete synchronous devctl driver callback fails only that devctl
  with an I/O error; its scratch slot stays quarantined (it may still be
  referenced) instead of stopping the IOP and the calling thread.
- a CPU exception stops that CPU only (previous commit); RPC, alarm, timer and
  interrupt callbacks that overrun are dropped and logged (earlier commits).

Kept, with reason: an exception escaping guest execution (H-IOP4 residual: it
leaves an instruction half done and device state unknown, so it now always
stops the emulator with the logged cause instead of being swallowed in the
unchecked path), a faulted/re-entered SPU device, 64-bit counter exhaustion,
a reset requested from inside guest execution, and freeing the code of an
alarm that is executing.

Diagnostics no longer bypass the host logger: the allocator trace and the
sceCdRE trace log through IopHost, and SPU bus faults report their cause
through the failure callback (H-IOP14 stderr item).

Tests: spu31 boundary checks now assert the bus-error contract (8 cases) and
the coalescing; ioman/device-callback tests assert the devctl contract; the
RPC boundaries assert an escaping exception outside an RPC.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
